  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nNot much has changed in the forecast. Upper level high pressure\nwill be the driving force behind our weather through at least the\ncoming weekend. The upper level ridge will begin to drift into \nsouthern New Mexico on Monday through Wednesday. That will be when\nwe will see our hottest temperatures of the week. A heat advisory\nis out for the lower Rio Grande Valley into Hudspeth County for \nMonday afternoon and evening. On Tuesday the upper level ridge \nwill be directly over southern New Mexico, so we will likely have \nto extended and possible expand the advisory. The ridge drifts\nonly a little to the west of Wednesday, so we may still need \nanother round of heat advisories for Wednesday afternoon.\n\nBy Thursday and Friday, the upper level ridge will be centered to\nour west in eastern Arizona. This will allow a little moisture to\nslip into the region from the east. The circulation around the \nupper level ridge may bring a vort max across the area so most of \nthe area will have at least a slight chance for rain on Thursday. \nHigh temperatures both days will drop a few degrees, but most of \nthe lowlands will continue to see triple digit heat. For the \nweekend, the upper level ridge will recenter itself over central \nNew Mexico, that ridge position will again limit our rain chances\nwhich will mainly be confined to area mountains. Triple digit \nlowland heat looks to continue next weekend. No relief from this \nlate summer heat as the extended models continue to show the upper\nlevel ridge anchored over the desert southwest through the middle\nof next week.",
